-- **Ignore All in the right-click spelling menu**, above Add to Dictionary, on a word the spelling checker has marked. Every occurrence of the word in that document loses its red line, including occurrences typed, pasted or brought back by Undo later, until the document is closed or PicoMarQ exits. Nothing is saved: the Markdown file and the dictionary are untouched, and the document is not marked as changed.
-  - *What counts as the same word.* Whole words only ("Qarths" stays marked when "Qarth" is ignored). A possessive counts as its word, with a straight or a curly apostrophe. Capitals follow the dictionary's rule: an ignored lower-case word is also ignored with a capital first letter and in capitals; an ignored capitalized word is also ignored in capitals, but not in lower case.
-  - *How.* Chromium cannot ignore a word, only add it to the dictionary. It leaves a word unmarked when the word starts inside an element with spell checking off, so `src/components/editor/IgnoredSpelling.ts` wraps the first letter of every occurrence in such an element, as a display decoration. The list is kept per document in the window's memory (`src/lib/ignored-spelling.ts`).
-  - *Other red lines.* Wrapping makes ProseMirror rewrite the text beside the wrapper, and Chromium drops the marks in rewritten text. The editor therefore asks for a full recheck (the 1.2.7 mechanism) at once after Ignore All, and one second after editing pauses when an ignored word was typed in a paragraph of more than 900 characters or changed by something other than typing (Undo, Replace, a paste). During a recheck every red line is gone for about a second. Typing an ignored word in a shorter paragraph needs no recheck: Chromium rechecks that paragraph by itself.
-  - *The caret.* Chromium runs its background checks only while the caret is at a place where spell checking is on. Right after the first letter of an ignored word, and before it at the start of a paragraph, the caret would be inside the wrapper, so there the selection is rewritten to name the same place from the neighboring text. The caret does not move and the arrow keys step as before.
-- **Settings > Spelling > Clear Custom Dictionary…** (a new last section of Settings) removes every word added with Add to Dictionary. A native question lists the words (the first 20, then "and N more") with Clear and Cancel; Cancel is the default and Esc cancels. An empty dictionary is reported without a question. The removed words are marked as misspelled again in every open document, which takes a recheck of each (`desktop/dictionary.cjs`, `clear_custom_dictionary` in `desktop/main.cjs`).
-- **Help > About PicoMarQ**, a fourth menu (Alt+H): the version, "Based on Scratch by Eric Li and contributors.", and buttons that open https://github.com/Crotalus65/PicoMarq and https://github.com/erictli/scratch in the browser (`desktop/about.cjs`). No personal name.
-- **A recheck waits until it can work** (`src/lib/spelling.ts`). Chromium checks only the editable text that holds the caret, and only text that is displayed; asked at another moment it clears every red line and brings none back until the caret next moves. A recheck now waits while the editor is hidden (Settings, the Markdown source view) and while the caret is outside it (the Find panel). This matters for Clear Custom Dictionary, which is always used with the document hidden behind Settings; without it the document came back with no red lines at all. The rechecks of 1.2.7 (paste, drop, return from the source view) go through the same code.
