+- **Find's highlights no longer take away red spelling lines.** Through 1.3.1 every paragraph in which Find highlighted a match lost its red lines, and they did not come back when Find was closed.
+  - *Why.* The highlights were ProseMirror decorations: an element around every match. To put an element around part of a paragraph, ProseMirror splits the paragraph's text into pieces, and Chromium drops the spelling marks of text that is rewritten. Closing Find rewrote the text once more.
+  - *How.* The matches are now painted with the CSS Custom Highlight API (`src/components/editor/SearchHighlight.ts`). Chromium is given a list of ranges and paints `::highlight()` behind them; the elements and the text of the document are not touched. The colors are the ones used before. In screenshots of 1.3.1 and 1.3.2 with the same search, the four highlighted rectangles have the same position, size and color.
+  - *Editing with Find open.* The matches are kept as positions and mapped through every change (`src/lib/search-matches.ts`): a highlight stays on its word when text is typed before it, grows with text typed inside it, and goes when its text is deleted, while the others keep their places in Find's count. The decorations behaved the same way.
+- **Text put in by Replace and Replace All is spell-checked.** Both now ask for the full recheck that 1.2.7 introduced for pasted text. A recheck made while the caret is in the Find panel would clear every red line and bring none back, so, as since 1.3.1, it waits until the caret is in the document again (Esc, or a click in the text). Until then the paragraphs that Replace changed show no red lines; the other paragraphs keep theirs.
+- **A recheck no longer waits for the mouse** (`drawFrames` in `src/lib/spelling.ts`). Chromium does a recheck as idle work that starts about a second after it is asked for. In a window in which nothing moves, and with text selected there is not even a blinking caret, that work sometimes did not start: every red line outside the caret's paragraph stayed away until the mouse moved or a key was pressed. Measured on 1.3.1 with text selected: 19 of 48 rechecks. A trace showed that Chromium's whole-document check had not been run, and two frames drawn by hand started it at once. The editor now keeps the window drawing frames for two seconds after it asks for a recheck (longer for a long document: ten seconds for 2 MB). With that, 160 of 160 rechecks completed (with a caret and with a selection, after typing, deleting and Undo). The fault was in every version since 1.2.7 (paste, return from the Markdown source view) and in 1.3.1's Ignore All and Clear Custom Dictionary. It was found now because the Find test rechecks after an Undo, which leaves text selected.
