+
+**Found while testing, the same in earlier versions, and not changed.**
+
+- *Changes that are not typed can still take red lines away.* The spelling marks belong to a piece of text and go when the editor rewrites that piece instead of letting Chromium type into it. A full recheck brings every line back: a paste, Ignore All, Replace, a visit to the Markdown source view and back (Ctrl+Shift+M twice), or closing and reopening the document. Each case below was tried three times.
+  - *In a long paragraph* (1,672 characters, about 300 words, with a misspelling 800 characters before the caret and one 820 characters after it) both red lines were lost every time, and more typing did not bring them back, after: typing an apostrophe, a quotation mark, two hyphens or three periods with Smart Typography on (the conversion is such a rewrite); Bold or Italic on a word; Undo; Enter in the middle of the paragraph. With Smart Typography off, typing those characters lost nothing. Typing letters, Backspace, and a paste lost nothing. By its source, Chromium rechecks by itself a whole paragraph of up to 1,024 characters around the caret, and otherwise 512 characters on either side, so this concerns paragraphs longer than about 170 words, away from the caret.
+  - *In a short paragraph* (77 characters) the red lines were kept, or back within three seconds, after everything tried (typing, deleting, cutting, pasting, Undo and Redo of typing, Bold, Enter, joining two paragraphs, making a heading, a list item or a quotation, the Smart Typography conversions), with three exceptions: Undo of a deleted selection (lost 3 times of 3), Bold switched on and off again (3 of 3), and Italic (1 of 3). There the lines came back when a letter was typed in the paragraph.
+  - Edit > Apply Smart Typography takes the red lines from the paragraphs it changes, except close to the caret (3 of 3 with the caret in another paragraph).
+- *Find looks for exactly the characters typed.* A straight apostrophe or quotation mark typed in the Find box does not find the curly one that Smart Typography puts into the text: `don't` does not find `don’t`, in either view. In the formatted view a phrase is not found where the formatting changes inside it: with `the *Odyssey* in` in the text, "the Odyssey" is not found, "Odyssey" is. Capital and small letters count as the same.
+- *Find counts, and Replace All replaces, at most 500 matches at a time.* In a document with 1,200 occurrences of a word, Find shows "1/500", and one Replace All replaces the first 500; Find then shows "1/500" again for the next ones. It took three Replace All commands to replace them all, in either view.
+- *The formatted view writes Markdown its own way.* A file is rewritten as a whole when it is saved after an edit in the formatted view (the Markdown source view saves exactly what is in it). 72 small files from other sources were opened, edited without changing anything, and saved. Text, headings, emphasis, quotations, links, images, lists, front matter and math came back with the same meaning, in PicoMarQ's notation: `*` for italics, `-` for list items, `#` headings, `---` rules, backtick fences, inline links for reference-style links, Unix line endings, no byte order mark, single blank lines, and no line break at the end of the file. These changed in meaning:
+  - Footnotes and citations in Pandoc's notation get backslashes: `[^1]` is saved as `\[^1\]`, `[@smith2020]` as `\[@smith2020\]`, `^[note]` as `^\[note\]`. PicoMarQ shows them as plain text either way, but other programs no longer read them as footnotes or citations. A command such as `\newpage` on a line of its own is saved as `\\newpage`, and a subscript `H~2~O` as `H~~2~~O` (struck through).
+  - A code block fenced with tildes (`~~~`) is not shown and is gone from the saved file. Code blocks fenced with backticks or indented are kept.
+  - HTML is dropped: a comment disappears, and tags such as `<sub>`, `<span>`, `<kbd>` or `<div>` are removed while the text inside them stays. `<u>` and `<br>` are understood. The entity `&nbsp;` becomes an ordinary space; other entities (`&amp;`, `&mdash;`) are shown and saved as written. A non-breaking space character is kept.
+  - In tables, the alignment of columns (`:---:`, `---:`) is lost. A link around an image is lost. Two paragraphs in one list item become one, and blank lines between list items go.
